# Outlier screen — triage, not exclusion
#   Current script: analysis/outlier_screen.py (pandas, numpy, matplotlib)
#   Earlier draft without the heatmap: analysis/outlier_screen1.py
#   Notes on the design: analysis/Outlier detection.odt
#   Within-subject percent change from that subject's still scan:
#     100 * (motion - still) / still
#   Separate contrasts: still→mild (19 pairs) and still→moderate (20 pairs)
#   Six locked measures only:
#     bilateral mean thickness, rostral middle frontal, superior temporal,
#     temporal pole, caudate (L+R), hippocampus (L+R)
#   Inclusion rule for the screen: Iglewicz–Hoaglin modified z
#     |0.6745 * (x - median) / MAD| > 3.5
#     median and MAD taken inside that measure and that contrast
#   Tukey 1.5×IQR and ENIGMA-style z (±2.698) are extra columns.
#   They are not a second way to drop a scan.
#   Surface-hole count from aseg is copied onto each row and is not part of the flag.
#   Outputs (26 Sep):
#     tables/outlier_screen.csv        234 rows (6 × 19 mild + 6 × 20 moderate)
#     tables/outlier_screen_pivot.csv  subject × condition
#     tables/outlier_screen_heatmap.png

# What the screen found (flags = re-review list)
#   Robust flags: 7 of 234. Tukey flags: 15. ENIGMA flags: 4.
#   Median percent change, mild / moderate:
#     mean thickness          −2.81% / −3.40%
#     rostral middle frontal  −2.10% / −0.02%
#     superior temporal       −3.13% / −5.51%
#     temporal pole           −9.18% / −20.85%   largest drift; not itself a flag
#     caudate (L+R)           +0.06% / +0.36%
#     hippocampus (L+R)       +0.73% / +0.84%
#   A shift shared by the sample does not flag. Modified z is computed inside
#   one measure and one contrast, so temporal pole can move ~21% and still
#   sit near the median of that column.
